---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'version', label: 'Version conflict' },
  { id: 'missing', label: 'Missing claim' },
  { id: 'interval', label: 'Interval mismatch' },
  { id: 'source', label: 'Inspect the evidence' },
];

const cases = [
  {
    id: 'version',
    number: '01',
    title: 'The numbers matched. The dataset version did not.',
    caseId: 's16_condition_label_01',
    problem: 'The requested analysis named dataset version fresh-panel-8-0-v2. The submitted report named fresh-panel-8-0-v1. Its reported Welch numbers matched the reference for the supplied data.',
    result: 'The correct five-field report records a dataset_version conflict and marks the overall submission MISMATCH, even though its numerical field is MATCH.',
    observations: [
      ['B · General Python', 'MATCH', 'MISMATCH', 'MATCH'],
      ['W · Stable wrapper', 'MATCH', 'MATCH', 'MISMATCH'],
      ['C · Bound verifier', 'MISMATCH', 'MISMATCH', 'MISMATCH'],
    ],
    detail: 'In the first run, both B and W explicitly listed the version conflict but still returned overall: MATCH. C listed the same conflict and returned overall: MISMATCH. The other repetitions show that B and W could also get the overall verdict right.',
  },
  {
    id: 'missing',
    number: '02',
    title: 'A missing standard error changed the answer.',
    caseId: 's16_missing_01',
    problem: 'The submitted Welch report omitted mean_difference_standard_error. Other numerical claims were present.',
    result: 'The expected report names the missing field and returns UNVERIFIABLE for both the numerical and overall assessment. It does not fill in the absent claim from a fresh calculation.',
    observations: [
      ['B · General Python', 'MATCH', 'No report', 'MATCH'],
      ['W · Stable wrapper', 'No report', 'No report', 'No report'],
      ['C · Bound verifier', 'UNVERIFIABLE', 'UNVERIFIABLE', 'UNVERIFIABLE'],
    ],
    detail: 'In two B runs, the final report listed no missing fields and returned MATCH. C named the absent standard error in each run. “No report” means the run did not yield an accepted five-field report; it is not an incorrect MATCH verdict. The study did not give B and W the complete canonical field vocabulary, which limits how their behavior on missing-field cases should be interpreted.',
  },
  {
    id: 'interval',
    number: '03',
    title: 'One confidence-interval endpoint escaped review.',
    caseId: 's16_single_04',
    problem: 'The submitted Welch report included an incorrect upper endpoint for its 95% confidence interval while the other reported fields matched the reference.',
    result: 'The expected report identifies mean_difference_ci_high as the single mismatched field and returns MISMATCH for the numerical and overall assessment.',
    observations: [
      ['B · General Python', 'MATCH', 'MATCH', 'MISMATCH'],
      ['W · Stable wrapper', 'MATCH', 'MATCH', 'No report'],
      ['C · Bound verifier', 'MISMATCH', 'MISMATCH', 'MISMATCH'],
    ],
    detail: 'In the first two runs, B and W accepted the submission as MATCH. C identified the upper endpoint in all three runs. B got the complete report right on its third run; W produced no accepted report on that run.',
  },
] as const;
---

<PageLayout
  title="Three Welch report cases from the evaluation"
  description="Inspect three fixed-panel Welch-report cases where general tools and a bound verifier returned different complete assessments, with all repetitions and evidence links."
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evaluation cases</p>
    <h1>What a plausible report can miss</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      A correct calculation is only part of checking a submitted result. These three
      cases show where the complete assessment changed when the reporting conditions,
      a required claim, or one interval endpoint was checked.
    </p>
    <p class="scope">
      From a developer-led study of 72 <strong>constructed</strong> Welch-report questions,
      using <code>gpt-4.1-mini-2025-04-14</code>. B, W, and C were separate sessions
      on the same inputs, each repeated three times. They are not a transcript in
      which nomue intercepted an error made by B or W.
    </p>
  </header>

  {cases.map((item) => (
    <section id={item.id} class="case-section">
      <p class="case-number">Case {item.number} · <code>{item.caseId}</code></p>
      <h2>{item.title}</h2>
      <div class="case-story">
        <div>
          <h3>Submitted report</h3>
          <p>{item.problem}</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Expected assessment</h3>
          <p>{item.result}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3>Observed overall verdicts</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Configuration</th><th scope="col">Run 1</th><th scope="col">Run 2</th><th scope="col">Run 3</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {item.observations.map((row) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{row[0]}</th><td>{row[1]}</td><td>{row[2]}</td><td>{row[3]}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="detail">{item.detail}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section id="source" class="case-section evidence">
    <h2>Inspect the evidence</h2>
    <p>
      These are selected illustrations from the published fixed panel, not a sample
      of everyday research submissions or an estimate of real-world error rates.
      B used general Python, W added a stable Welch calculation wrapper, and C used
      a bound verifier that returned the completed five-field assessment. Binding,
      report construction, and the structured return changed together in C.
    </p>
    <p>
      The original study scored complete five-field reports: B returned 132/216,
      W returned 131/216, and C returned 216/216 expected reports. C's result path
      was deterministic and confirmed before the scored run. The comparison does
      not isolate a binding-only effect or establish nomue's accuracy on real
      research submissions, other methods, or other models.
    </p>
    <p>
      To inspect these cases, download the replication package from Zenodo and
      search their case IDs in <code>INPUTS.json</code>, <code>EXPECTED.json</code>,
      and the 648 rows of <code>RESULT.sanitized.json</code>. Run
      <code>python reproduce.py</code> from the extracted package to rescore every
      saved report. <code>ANSWERS.json</code> retains final messages needed for the
      study's output-format sensitivity analysis.
    </p>
    <p>
      The public archive supports rescoring saved evidence. It does not contain
      the private C verifier or complete provider transcripts, so it does not
      independently rerun the original sessions. These cases are not public
      nomue Records for the local Release 1 verifier.
    </p>
    <p class="actions">
      <a href="https://zenodo.org/records/22784595">Download the source package</a>
      <a href="/evaluation/">Read the study design and limits</a>
      <a href="/docs/">Explore current nomue capabilities</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .page-head { padding-bottom: 2.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .page-head h1 { max-width: 20ch; margin: 0.9rem 0 1.25rem; font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.6rem); }
  .standfirst { max-width: 46rem; font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .scope { max-width: 47rem; margin: 2rem 0 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--gold); background: var(--wash-hi); font-size: 0.96rem; }
  .case-section { padding: 3rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint); }
  .case-number { margin: 0 0 0.75rem; color: var(--gold-ink); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .case-section h2 { max-width: 28ch; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.25rem); }
  .case-section h3 { margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.08rem; }
  .case-story { display: grid; gap: 1rem; margin: 1.75rem 0; }
  .case-story > div { padding: 1rem 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .case-story h3 { margin-top: 0; }
  .case-story p { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { width: 100%; min-width: 35rem; border-collapse: collapse; }
  th, td { text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint); }
  .detail { max-width: 50rem; margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .evidence { border-bottom: 0; }
  .evidence > p { max-width: 50rem; }
  .actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem 1.5rem; }
  @media (min-width: 760px) { .case-story { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
</style>
